Below is a professionally drafted **Scope of Services** which you may use in your engagement letter or proposal. The language is structured, practical, and aligned with real-life tax litigation and compliance handling before FBR and Provincial Revenue Authorities.

---

### **Scope of Services**  
**Review and Support for Open Tax Matters**

This engagement shall cover professional assistance in respect of pending tax matters relating to income tax, federal sales tax, and provincial sales tax before the relevant tax authorities, including the Commissioner Inland Revenue (Appeals) and the Appellate Tribunal Inland Revenue.

#### **1. Identification and Review of Open Matters**

The Management shall provide a comprehensive list of all open matters, including:

- Pending notices issued by tax authorities;  
- Ongoing assessment or audit proceedings;  
- Appeals pending before Commissioner (Appeals);  
- Appeals pending before the Appellate Tribunal Inland Revenue;  
- Any recovery proceedings or related correspondence.

Upon receipt of the above, we shall conduct a structured review of each matter to understand:

- Nature of tax exposure;  
- Relevant tax year(s) and statutory provisions involved;  
- Status of proceedings and statutory deadlines;  
- Contentions previously taken by the existing tax consultant;  
- Financial and compliance implications.

---

#### **2. Information Mapping and Documentation Extraction**

For each open matter, we shall:

- Identify the specific information, reconciliations, explanations, and supporting documents required to effectively conclude or strengthen the case;  
- Prepare a detailed information request list (IRL) tailored to each matter;  
- Coordinate with Management to extract required records from available financial, accounting, and tax documentation;  
- Assist in organizing documentary evidence including tax returns, notices, replies, working papers, ledgers, contracts, invoices, payment records, and reconciliations.

This process will be carried out in close coordination with the Management to ensure factual accuracy and completeness of records.

---

#### **3. Gap Analysis and Technical Evaluation**

After compilation of the available documentation, we shall:

- Perform a technical review of records to identify gaps, inconsistencies, or deficiencies;  
- Evaluate whether the legal position already adopted is adequately supported by documentation;  
- Highlight weaknesses in existing contentions, if any;  
- Identify missing documentary support or alternative legal arguments;  
- Suggest corrective measures, supplementary explanations, or additional documentation required to strengthen the case.

This exercise will focus on improving defensibility before tax authorities and appellate forums.

---

#### **4. Arrangement and Handover to Existing Tax Consultant**

Upon completion of documentation and analysis:

- We shall compile matter-wise files (physical and/or soft copy);  
- Arrange documentation in a structured manner suitable for submission before authorities;  
- Prepare a summary note for each open item outlining facts, legal position, risk exposure, and documentation status;  
- Share the compiled record and findings with the existing tax consultant for onward representation before the tax authorities.

---

#### **5. Team Structure and Supervision**

The assignment shall be executed by:

- **One Job In-Charge**, responsible for day-to-day coordination, documentation review, and preparation of analysis;  
- **One Supporting Staff Member**, responsible for data compilation, reconciliation, and documentation arrangement;  
- **Supervision by an Experienced Tax Expert**, who shall provide technical oversight, review legal positions, guide on risk areas, and ensure quality control of deliverables.

All technical conclusions and recommendations shall be reviewed and approved by the supervising tax expert prior to finalization.

---

#### **6. Nature and Limitation of Services**

This engagement is limited to review, documentation support, and gap identification in respect of open matters. Representation before tax authorities, drafting of appeals, or court litigation shall be undertaken only if separately agreed.

---

If you would like, I can also prepare:

- A **commercial proposal version** with fee structure and timelines;  
- A more concise version suitable for inclusion in an engagement letter; or  
- A premium corporate version tailored for large groups with multiple entities and jurisdictions.

I have carefully reviewed the attached notice issued under section 176(1) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 2001 for Tax Year 2022 in the case of Prime Global Energies Limited. fileciteturn0file0  

This is not an assessment order. It is a post–Federal Constitutional Court judgment information notice, specifically seeking determination of super tax applicability under section 4C (read with the Fifth Schedule) in light of paragraph (viii) of the Court’s short order dated 27-01-2026.

The key substance of the notice is as follows:

1. The Court has upheld super tax under sections 4B and 4C as intra vires.

2. However, in case of Exploration & Production (E&P) companies, the levy must:
   - Remain subject to Rules 4AA and 4AB of the Fifth Schedule;
   - Be read together with the relevant Petroleum Concession Agreements (PCAs);
   - Not exceed the aggregate tax rate / ceiling prescribed under Rule 4 of the Fifth Schedule or under the PCA.

3. The Department is required to:
   - Undertake PCA-wise determination;
   - Juxtapose PCA terms with the Regulation of Mines & Minerals (Government Control) Act, 1948;
   - Examine which Income Tax law governs the PCA (1922 Act / 1979 Ordinance / 2001 Ordinance);
   - Restrict computation to income under Rule 1 of the Fifth Schedule;
   - Ensure that super tax does not breach the threshold unless there is express legislative intent.

4. Before any recovery, the Commissioner must first determine liability and provide an opportunity of hearing.

The notice specifically calls for:

- Identification of applicable tax regime for each PCA;
- Computation of aggregate tax incidence under the Fifth Schedule (income tax, surcharge, super tax);
- Segregated working of income under Rule 1 of the Fifth Schedule, reconciled with return;
- Demonstration (with documentary evidence) whether section 4B / 4C causes breach of aggregate tax ceiling, and quantification of such excess.

It further warns that in case of non-compliance, determination will be finalized based on available record strictly in accordance with section 4C and Fifth Schedule.

In essence, this is a fact-finding and computation-driven notice following the Constitutional Court ruling. The Department is placing the burden on the taxpayer to demonstrate that super tax breaches the contractual or statutory ceiling under the PCA read with the Fifth Schedule.

Strategically, the response will need to be technical and PCA-specific. The core battlefield will be:
- What constitutes “income under Rule 1”;
- How aggregate tax incidence is computed;
- Whether super tax forms part of the ceiling;
- Whether legislative intent expressly overrides PCA protection.

This notice signals that the Department is moving PCA-by-PCA rather than applying a blanket super tax position. That makes the computational structure and historical tax regime analysis absolutely critical.
